WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Welcome to Viggo Mortensen!

00:00:02.922 --> 00:00:04.062
Thanks.

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:10.740
Tell me, Viggo, how do you promote a film in these days,

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.797
you're in Madrid, but one usually travels around to promote a film?

00:00:15.797 --> 00:00:20.406
It's difficult. I just returned after

00:00:20.406 --> 00:00:25.706
almost a months road trip alone, because I didn't want to get sick.

00:00:25.706 --> 00:00:31.201
So it was a long trip, but it was very exciting.

00:00:31.201 --> 00:00:34.442
How did you experience driving through Europe now?

00:00:34.442 --> 00:00:35.561
It was strange.

00:00:35.561 --> 00:00:38.363
People are more attentive to

00:00:38.363 --> 00:00:40.761
the fact that life is short.

00:00:41.621 --> 00:00:44.331
That ...

00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:48.132
That life is frail.

00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:49.270
Difficult.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.183
But it's always been like that. Life is always uncertain.

00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:57.584
People are just more aware of it now.

00:00:57.584 --> 00:01:02.389
And I think that that's a good thing. It's good to face things, I think. 

00:01:02.389 --> 00:01:10.706
Is that something you yourself have been conscious of earlier?

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:17.303
I've always thought about death, actually.

00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:21.305
And that doesn't have to be negative. I can remember that my mother told me,

00:01:21.305 --> 00:01:25.180
I must have been two or three years old,

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:33.201
and I asked her after a dog died, and a horse, and some old people I had heard of

00:01:33.201 --> 00:01:35.667
in my family who had died, and I said «Mom»

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.386
«Are you going to die?»

00:01:39.338 --> 00:01:43.815
And she said: «Why are you asking that? We're not going to.» 

00:01:43.815 --> 00:01:45.293
«But I want to know, mom.»

00:01:45.293 --> 00:01:46.976
«Will you die?»

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.293
Then she said: «Yes. But it's not going to happen now. It'll be a long while.»

00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:53.680
And I said: «Well, okay.»

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.480
«And am I going to die?»

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.359
And she says:

00:01:57.359 --> 00:01:59.511
«Yes. But not now.»

00:01:59.511 --> 00:02:02.280
And I said: «Okay. I just want to know.»

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.319
And since that day

00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:08.507
I wake up every day, and that's the first thing I think about.

00:02:08.507 --> 00:02:14.460
«Mmm. Okay.» I wasn't afraid.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.426
But I thought that you just has to do something.

00:02:18.426 --> 00:02:21.499
You gotta get going. You have to do things, you know.

00:02:21.499 --> 00:02:23.869
So that's what I think about.

00:02:23.869 --> 00:02:27.656
But I think that most people don't think about death every day.

00:02:27.656 --> 00:02:30.329
And maybe they should.

00:02:30.329 --> 00:02:34.051
To appreciate the life that they have.

00:02:34.051 --> 00:02:40.553
The friends they have. The possibilities they have to communicate with people in a real way.

00:02:40.553 --> 00:02:45.587
If everyone thought about that, there wouldn't be so many problems in the world,

00:02:45.587 --> 00:02:47.844
there would be a little more compassion,

00:02:47.844 --> 00:02:51.867
and better communication.

00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:58.391
Gro, you are the oldest of us after all, what do you think about these things?

00:02:59.435 --> 00:03:06.205
I have not thought about death every day in the way he now describes. 

00:03:06.205 --> 00:03:08.459
And I mean throughout life.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:14.219
But I have noticed that after I turned 70,

00:03:14.219 --> 00:03:16.698
and not before that, I think, but after that

00:03:16.698 --> 00:03:20.603
I began thinking in the lines of:

00:03:20.603 --> 00:03:23.044
Now there's an era left in life.

00:03:23.044 --> 00:03:25.443
How long it is, you don't know.

00:03:25.443 --> 00:03:29.000
It can be five years, ten years, fifteen years.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.727
And that kind of thinking didn't occur until I was 70-75.

00:03:33.727 --> 00:03:38.644
So, he's special, that one, who thinks about that every day!

00:03:38.644 --> 00:03:42.363
Have you thought about where you want to be buried and such?

00:03:42.363 --> 00:03:43.511
No.

00:03:43.941 --> 00:03:45.580
Do you, Viggo?

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:52.456
You've lived all over the world, you're a man who's 

00:03:52.456 --> 00:03:55.763
seemingly rootless. Do you know where you want to end up?

00:03:56.377 --> 00:04:00.821
Yes, I have thought about it. And I have written it down.

00:04:00.821 --> 00:04:06.180
And I might change it, the idea.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:15.898
But what I want is for my ashes to be spread different places in the world.

00:04:15.898 --> 00:04:27.064
Lakes ... Streams ... In the ocean ... Different places where I've lived or that mean something to me in the world.

00:04:27.064 --> 00:04:29.589
And I want to put som money aside 

00:04:29.589 --> 00:04:33.903
for my son, or who it might be, but I think it will be my son,

00:04:33.903 --> 00:04:39.143
so that he can ... I've spoken to him about it, and he's ...

00:04:39.143 --> 00:04:41.263
He's okay with it.

00:04:41.263 --> 00:04:49.658
To take these ashes and spread them out, and he'll have enough money to stay at hotels and travel and such.

00:04:49.658 --> 00:04:52.619
There and there and there, in different places.

00:04:52.619 --> 00:04:59.524
That's my idea. And that might not be what happens, I don't know. But that's my idea right now.

00:04:59.524 --> 00:05:05.291
Let's hope that that won't happen for a long time. We have to talk about your film, which is called «Falling».

00:05:05.291 --> 00:05:08.764
You've directed it yourself, you've written it yourself, it's 

00:05:08.764 --> 00:05:11.630
a very personal story.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.257
How did you come up with wanting to write it? 

00:05:15.762 --> 00:05:18.364
While we're speaking of death ...

00:05:18.364 --> 00:05:22.561
I wrote the story ... The idea came

00:05:22.561 --> 00:05:24.843
after my mother's death.

00:05:24.843 --> 00:05:27.567
After her funeral.

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:38.666
As happens in funerals, you hear a lot of stories about the person who died.

00:05:38.666 --> 00:05:42.067
And I recognized some of those stories.

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:44.782
And then there were some new stories.

00:05:44.782 --> 00:05:53.182
But even the stories that I recognized were slightly different. It depended on who was talking about my mother.

00:05:53.182 --> 00:05:55.672
So I thought it was interesting.

00:05:55.672 --> 00:05:58.797
I think that memory or memories 

00:05:58.797 --> 00:06:03.720
are more a collection of feelings than facts.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.864
So I thought it was very interesting how

00:06:07.864 --> 00:06:12.991
it was slightly different depending on who told the stories about my mother.

00:06:12.991 --> 00:06:16.635
So I wrote these things down before I forgot them.

00:06:16.635 --> 00:06:19.114
And when I had a look at all of this,

00:06:19.114 --> 00:06:23.887
I thought: «This is a good story. It could become a good movie.»

00:06:23.887 --> 00:06:28.577
And then I started to write «Falling».

00:06:28.577 --> 00:06:38.421
The story isn't that different from your own family history.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:43.700
Among other things it's about a dad who

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.138
suffers from dementia.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:47.737
Yes.

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:55.616
You've had quite a lot of that in your family. Has that made you extra worried about the same thing happening to you?

00:06:55.616 --> 00:07:00.186
Yes. And I discovered that there's a test you can take

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:03.161
where you can see if you're prone to it.

00:07:04.666 --> 00:07:06.562
In your genes or something.

00:07:07.053 --> 00:07:08.608
And then ...

00:07:08.608 --> 00:07:15.580
There was a friend who told me: «You know that you can find out if you're prone to get dementia or Alzheimer's?»

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.283
«Have you done it?»

00:07:18.283 --> 00:07:20.113
And he said: «No, I don't want to know that.»

00:07:20.113 --> 00:07:21.830
And I said: «I want that.»

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.313
And I went to the doctor and I got a blood test.

00:07:26.313 --> 00:07:29.877
And they said: «No.»

00:07:29.877 --> 00:07:32.316
«I don't think you'll get it.»

00:07:32.316 --> 00:07:34.903
And I said: «Okay». Although you never know.

00:07:35.272 --> 00:07:37.944
I've had ...

00:07:37.944 --> 00:07:41.918
I've experienced the dementia process 

00:07:42.563 --> 00:07:45.004
very intimately.

00:07:45.004 --> 00:07:50.876
With both my mother and my father, my step-father, my grandparents ...

00:07:50.876 --> 00:07:55.526
And a lot of family members in both Denmark and the US. 

00:07:55.526 --> 00:08:01.630
So that helped me describe it, the illness, in the movie.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:10.498
And to create Willis, who is the old man in the movie, who portrays my father in the movie.

00:08:10.498 --> 00:08:13.719
And his relationship to his son

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:18.564
and the rest of the family. So that helped me describe that, yeah.

00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:24.095
What is it that people don't understand about that illness?

00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:33.715
When you've been close to it and helped look after it

00:08:34.268 --> 00:08:42.779
you can see that it's mostly those who look after the people who are sick,

00:08:42.779 --> 00:08:44.622
who become confused.

00:08:44.622 --> 00:08:50.216
It's not them who are confused. But when you watch films about dementia or Alzheimer's

00:08:50.216 --> 00:08:55.309
they normally show a human who's very confused.

00:08:55.862 --> 00:08:57.639
And ...

00:08:57.639 --> 00:09:02.982
And if they show the view point of the sick person

00:09:02.982 --> 00:09:06.690
they show a human that's confused.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.533
And that's not my experience.

00:09:09.885 --> 00:09:17.278
So I've tried to show that through images and sound

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.175
and the actor

00:09:23.188 --> 00:09:25.110
his viewpoint.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:27.177
And Willis isn't confused.

00:09:27.177 --> 00:09:31.774
It's the ones who take care of him. It's his son, who I play, and others, who become confused.

00:09:31.774 --> 00:09:34.867
But they see what they see and hear ...

00:09:34.867 --> 00:09:39.714
And the mistake you can make when you

00:09:39.714 --> 00:09:42.833
are with these people

00:09:42.833 --> 00:09:46.052
who have dementia is to correct them.

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:47.974
You want to correct them.

00:09:47.974 --> 00:09:49.803
For example.

00:09:49.803 --> 00:09:55.131
It's very normal that they want to talk about a person who's long gone.

00:09:55.131 --> 00:09:59.346
So you'll naturally

00:09:59.346 --> 00:10:06.606
want to say: «No, dad. Johnny died 30 years ago».

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:15.204
And if you do that, Johnny dies again for them. And it's terrible.

00:10:15.204 --> 00:10:20.996
And if they're clear enough to come out of it,

00:10:20.996 --> 00:10:24.370
then they feel really stupid.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:25.677
That doesn't help.

00:10:25.677 --> 00:10:29.313
Who is it that you want to help?

00:10:29.313 --> 00:10:32.006
It's not them. It's yourself.

00:10:32.006 --> 00:10:35.812
You want your father or your mother or your best friend

00:10:35.812 --> 00:10:38.797
as they were before, and that's not happening.

00:10:38.797 --> 00:10:41.907
You have to accept what it is.

00:10:41.907 --> 00:10:45.436
And for example, if my father has said:

00:10:45.436 --> 00:10:50.992
«I had breakfast with Johnny today.»

00:10:50.992 --> 00:10:52.909
«And we have a nice conversation.»

00:10:52.909 --> 00:10:56.763
I will say: «So, what did you eat?»

00:10:56.763 --> 00:10:59.770
And: «Was it tasty?» «How is Johnny doing?»

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:05.169
«What's happening?» «Nothing is happening.» And he's happy to talk about it. And then it's over.

00:11:05.169 --> 00:11:08.046
One shouldn't correct it.

00:11:08.046 --> 00:11:14.371
Your description fits very well with ... We spoke to

00:11:14.371 --> 00:11:18.626
the Swedish football manager Erik Hamrén who has a similar experience,

00:11:18.626 --> 00:11:21.333
and he agreed with you about this, very recently.

00:11:21.333 --> 00:11:25.060
Do you have any close experience with this, Gro?

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:28.598
The case was that my mother

00:11:28.598 --> 00:11:35.374
had this increasingly the last seven-eight years of her life.

00:11:35.374 --> 00:11:39.941
So I've experienced what he now talks about,

00:11:39.941 --> 00:11:45.735
that she thought that my father still ...

00:11:45.735 --> 00:11:48.707
She asked: «Where is Gubbe?»

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.255
And that was a little difficult because

00:11:53.255 --> 00:11:59.547
we then had to say that he had passed quite some years ago.

00:11:59.547 --> 00:12:04.498
And I completely understand what he is saying, because you don't have to correct them.

00:12:04.498 --> 00:12:07.474
But when you get a question, it becomes a bit more difficult.

00:12:07.474 --> 00:12:11.597
But I remember that, that she sometimes asked about her husband.

00:12:11.597 --> 00:12:13.054
My father.

00:12:13.054 --> 00:12:22.645
Your father was Danish, Viggo, as far as I know he died about three years ago.

00:12:22.645 --> 00:12:29.011
And in the middle of your huge career that you have, after all, were you able to be there for him?

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:30.955
Yes.

00:12:30.955 --> 00:12:36.043
I haven't made that many movies in the last years because of that.

00:12:36.043 --> 00:12:39.414
A movie every fourth year or something like that.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:48.022
Because I wanted to be with my mother and then my father, who died two years later.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.752
But you can also ... I know that

00:12:53.752 --> 00:12:59.613
as the prime minister says, that it's difficult when they ask you,

00:12:59.613 --> 00:13:03.780
but you just have to be quick, you just have to improvise.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.794
I can tell you another story about my father, he once asked ...

00:13:07.794 --> 00:13:13.656
He was used to saying, he lived in the US for many years.

00:13:13.656 --> 00:13:22.988
And normally we wouldn't speak Danish together unless we were talking about something we didn't want anyone else to hear.

00:13:22.988 --> 00:13:24.750
Or understand.

00:13:25.641 --> 00:13:27.804
A cryptolect!

00:13:27.804 --> 00:13:32.838
Yes! But when he got dementia, he began to speak Danish all the time.

00:13:32.838 --> 00:13:42.894
And with a strange accent that he must have had when he was young.

00:13:42.894 --> 00:13:45.961
A young man, or a boy.

00:13:45.961 --> 00:13:54.105
So he was speaking Danish the whole time, and there was a nurse who was with us, and she said in English:

00:13:54.105 --> 00:13:58.512
«I don't understand what he's saying, he's talking that language again, can you ask him what he wants?»

00:13:58.512 --> 00:14:00.947
I said: «Okay. What is it, father?»

00:14:00.947 --> 00:14:05.160
He said: «We can't let the elderly stand outside in the snow!»

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:11.460
And I said to the nurse: «It's okay, it's not important, I can deal with it.»

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.940
«What are you talking about, dad?» And then I discovered that

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:19.525
continued saying: «The old people are outside in the snow, it's not good.»

00:14:19.525 --> 00:14:20.962
«We have to get them.»

00:14:20.962 --> 00:14:24.647
«They're at the station. Iustrup station.»

00:14:24.647 --> 00:14:29.149
And there was a station until 

00:14:30.408 --> 00:14:34.111
40 years ago

00:14:34.111 --> 00:14:36.332
in Ustrup, but it doesn't exist any longer.

00:14:36.332 --> 00:14:43.200
And I knew that there were people in the family who had traveled from an island called Samsø and they had taken the train,

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:47.096
a long travel to come visit them.

00:14:47.096 --> 00:14:51.419
And they were to be picked up by a horse carriage at the station.

00:14:51.419 --> 00:14:54.859
And I said: «Okay, dad, I'll be right back, I'm going to pick them up.»

00:14:54.859 --> 00:14:58.356
And I went to the kitchen and grabbed a cup of coffee.

00:14:58.356 --> 00:15:02.041
And a couple of minutes later I went back to him.

00:15:02.041 --> 00:15:07.160
And I thought: «Either, he's forgotten it, or he'll ask about it again.»

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:13.607
So I went back to him with a cup of coffee, and he said: «So?»

00:15:13.607 --> 00:15:15.290
And I said:

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:22.058
«I have picked them up. But they're so tired from the long travel that they've gone to bed.»

00:15:22.058 --> 00:15:27.847
«They apologize. But we'll meet them for breakfast.»

00:15:27.847 --> 00:15:30.598
«Completely okay. Very good. Thank you for that.»

00:15:30.598 --> 00:15:31.928
And then it's over.

00:15:31.928 --> 00:15:33.368
But if I had said:

00:15:33.368 --> 00:15:37.507
«There's no station in Ustrup and they're dead.»

00:15:37.507 --> 00:15:42.908
He would experience that again, and that's terrible. And that's not necessary, that's what I'm saying.

00:15:42.908 --> 00:15:44.760
You should improvise, you should be quick.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:51.456
Viggo, you grew up partly with your father, partly with your mother.

00:15:51.456 --> 00:15:56.577
Partly in the US, in South America, in Denmark ...

00:15:56.577 --> 00:16:03.701
And you now live with your beloved in Madrid.

00:16:04.192 --> 00:16:04.914
Yes.

00:16:04.914 --> 00:16:10.453
Has this made you rootless, or has it just made you adaptable, what do you think?

00:16:11.957 --> 00:16:18.763
I think that every human being sometimes feels

00:16:20.575 --> 00:16:24.815
strange or that they don't belong anywhere.

00:16:24.815 --> 00:16:30.240
Even when you've just lived in a village your entire life. So that's just

00:16:31.284 --> 00:16:34.169
the way we are. But what I've learned

00:16:34.169 --> 00:16:41.982
from traveling so much in my life, is that it's more important how you feel

00:16:43.149 --> 00:16:44.626
than where you are.

00:16:45.302 --> 00:16:50.000
You can feel at home and communicate with people everywhere.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:51.799
With all kinds of people

00:16:51.799 --> 00:16:54.135
I think, that's what I've learned.

00:16:54.135 --> 00:16:57.588
Of course I have special

00:16:58.509 --> 00:17:03.685
connections to Denmark, to Scandinavia,

00:17:03.685 --> 00:17:07.820
I also have a connection to Norway, I have family there,

00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:14.033
who have lived in Oslo and in Trondheim, and I've traveled a lot in Norway,

00:17:14.033 --> 00:17:20.000
so I cherish Norway and Norway's landscape,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:27.172
and I have a strong connection to Argentina, where I lived the first ten years of my life.

00:17:27.172 --> 00:17:32.519
And the US, of course, where I was born and where I have family.

00:17:32.519 --> 00:17:36.177
But there are many places where I 

00:17:36.177 --> 00:17:38.243
feel at home.

00:17:38.243 --> 00:17:41.986
And I think I can feel at home everywhere, it's possible.

00:17:41.986 --> 00:17:49.695
We're speaking Norwegian and Danish and Scandinavian languages here, but does your

00:17:49.695 --> 00:17:57.070
personality, and I'll ask Marcus about this as well, but when you can switch languages like this,

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:02.072
does the personality come with? Does the personality change at the same time?

00:18:03.393 --> 00:18:06.405
Well, that depends ... I think,

00:18:06.405 --> 00:18:12.603
and people have said that to me, that I notice it the most when I write poems

00:18:13.678 --> 00:18:18.165
in Spanish or in English, there are some things I can express better.

00:18:18.165 --> 00:18:22.127
It depends on what the subject of my writing is.

00:18:22.127 --> 00:18:28.395
Sometimes it's easier to express something in Spanish, and sometimes it's easier in English, for example. 

00:18:28.395 --> 00:18:31.287
But when I speak

00:18:32.239 --> 00:18:36.047
Spanish or Italian, for example,

00:18:36.047 --> 00:18:42.511
I have this body language. I'm a little more ...

00:18:42.511 --> 00:18:45.982
I speak with my hands and things like that, without thinking about it.

00:18:45.982 --> 00:18:52.694
And I might do that now as well, when I'm speaking Danish, maybe it's because I grew up in Argentina, I don't know!

00:18:52.694 --> 00:18:55.402
But I can tell that 

00:18:56.293 --> 00:18:59.503
language in some way changes the way I

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.913
present myself. I think so.

00:19:03.913 --> 00:19:12.472
What about you Marcus? Do you become someone else when you speak English instead of Swedish? Or when you speak Ethiopian?

00:19:14.898 --> 00:19:19.598
First and foremost I think it's fantastic what Viggo is talking about because 

00:19:19.598 --> 00:19:25.877
we need to hear more stories about how we as human beings can fit in almost anywhere.

00:19:25.877 --> 00:19:32.173
You can live a full life and you can never judge anyone.

00:19:32.173 --> 00:19:36.881
If Viggo hadn't been famous around the world,

00:19:36.881 --> 00:19:41.002
who would have thought that he speaks Spanish and understands the culture of Argentina? 

00:19:41.002 --> 00:19:46.124
Who, when they look at me, would think that I'm rooted in Swedish and that I'm Ethiopian?

00:19:46.124 --> 00:19:48.975
You should never judge anyone, and I think it's magical to hear this.

00:19:48.975 --> 00:19:52.081
I've lived in seven different countries.

00:19:52.511 --> 00:19:55.765
And I was really equally happy in all seven.

00:19:55.765 --> 00:20:02.172
I've lived in Japan. I've lived in France. Switzerland, Austria, Sweden, USA.

00:20:02.172 --> 00:20:06.768
And you're quite quick to apply yourself.

00:20:06.768 --> 00:20:14.334
You want to get something out of the day, you want to learn a lot and you want to be social. 

00:20:14.334 --> 00:20:18.006
And that's the magic thing about people, we can do that.

00:20:18.006 --> 00:20:20.926
When I was 18 years old and in Japan alone,

00:20:20.926 --> 00:20:24.095
wow! How would I survive? How would I take the subway?

00:20:24.095 --> 00:20:27.091
It took me two days to figure it out, and I loved it!

00:20:27.091 --> 00:20:30.157
And I lived in France ...

00:20:30.157 --> 00:20:35.812
And we as human beings are quick ... We have a magic,

00:20:35.812 --> 00:20:40.582
that we can apply ourselves. And then you also see that 

00:20:40.582 --> 00:20:46.459
all the other things we usually judge people by, are you Muslim, are you Jewish, are you a Christian, 

00:20:46.459 --> 00:20:50.102
all of that becomes secondary. It's all about human reaction. 

00:20:50.102 --> 00:20:54.581
How are we? And how do we communicate with this fellow human being.

00:20:54.581 --> 00:20:59.205
So we need more stories like this, and we need to not be afraid ...

00:20:59.205 --> 00:21:05.518
I think it's also associated with the fact that Viggo writes and does creative things,

00:21:05.518 --> 00:21:09.078
so seeing that you don't have to be afraid of death

00:21:09.078 --> 00:21:12.889
is something he can apply to his job.

00:21:12.889 --> 00:21:16.740
It's the same with food, to me, food has a spiritual anchoring.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:24.597
If I'm with my Ethiopian family, where you first and foremost eat with your hands, you offer food with your hands,

00:21:24.597 --> 00:21:29.537
that creates a much more reactive and engaging meal.

00:21:29.537 --> 00:21:32.316
And if I were to do that with my Swedish family,

00:21:32.316 --> 00:21:34.644
it wouldn't be appropriate, maybe.

00:21:34.644 --> 00:21:40.475
But it's the opposite, we need to eat more with our hands in Sweden!

00:21:41.826 --> 00:21:46.415
You of course have to wash your hands, corona poster.

00:21:46.998 --> 00:21:50.905
The more we learn about what other people do, the richer life becomes! 

00:21:52.410 --> 00:22:00.068
Viggo, your story is both a story about a family, but

00:22:00.068 --> 00:22:03.085
it's about a division in a family, really.

00:22:03.914 --> 00:22:11.226
And it's also an image of what was your home country before you moved to Madrid, USA. 

00:22:11.872 --> 00:22:18.679
Am I right about that, is it a small image of a country, in a way?

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:27.899
Yeah. I think that any story about my family in a book or in a movie

00:22:29.496 --> 00:22:35.933
can be a reflection ... You can see society in it, you can.

00:22:35.933 --> 00:22:49.800
And in «Falling» you certainly can, because there's conflict and polarization in the family that reflects what's happening in society in US. But not just in the US.

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:55.284
There's something called the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:22:55.284 --> 00:22:58.895
But there's also another kind of pandemic.

00:22:58.895 --> 00:23:03.093
Which is as old as the human race.

00:23:03.093 --> 00:23:12.849
And that's a pandemic that's on the rise sometimes, and it's on the rise right now, and it's the pandemic of bad communication.

00:23:12.849 --> 00:23:17.010
The bad communication pandemic.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:20.668
That's happening now. It's just as high up a problem

00:23:20.668 --> 00:23:23.918
and it's not just the United Stated, it's in Europe,

00:23:23.918 --> 00:23:29.897
you have people who feel free now to be openly racist and 

00:23:29.897 --> 00:23:32.735
misogynist and intolerant

00:23:32.735 --> 00:23:38.593
and of course, this example is set by our leaders, often.

00:23:38.593 --> 00:23:47.314
You know. And they give people permission to behave this way. The problem is that people are unable to communicate well.

00:23:47.314 --> 00:23:52.849
And you can look at the conditions in the movie and compare it to what is happening in the US,

00:23:52.849 --> 00:23:56.354
and maybe more this year than a year ago.

00:23:56.354 --> 00:24:03.270
That's for sure. But I hope that, just as in the movie,

00:24:04.314 --> 00:24:18.445
I hope that memories of family connections,

00:24:18.445 --> 00:24:22.065
the memory of a time when we could connect,

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:29.659
might help us to reconnect now. This is what happens in the story, with the family.

00:24:29.659 --> 00:24:35.714
And I think in society, whether it's the United States or Norway or Denmark or Ethiopia or anywhere,

00:24:35.714 --> 00:24:38.286
when people remember,

00:24:41.725 --> 00:24:46.304
you know, any country, Argentina, Somalia, anywhere, it doesn't matter,

00:24:46.304 --> 00:24:55.936
if people together make an effort to remember moments in their society's history when there was a good communication

00:24:55.936 --> 00:24:57.816
and some give and take

00:24:58.707 --> 00:25:00.707
then they can re-establish that again.

00:25:00.707 --> 00:25:03.297
I'm optimistic in that way.

00:25:03.297 --> 00:25:07.711
I think it's always possible to communicate.

00:25:07.711 --> 00:25:11.994
In «Falling», it's mainly about a father and a son.

00:25:13.007 --> 00:25:17.759
And the son is very stubborn.

00:25:18.865 --> 00:25:22.804
He wants to communicate with his father.

00:25:22.804 --> 00:25:25.673
And the father doesn't want to communicate with him.

00:25:25.673 --> 00:25:28.590
Doesn't want to see him, doesn't want to accept him.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:37.279
And it's possible, even if you're just as stubborn about communicating as the other person is stubborn about not communicating,

00:25:37.279 --> 00:25:40.975
maybe you will never have a point of contact.

00:25:40.975 --> 00:25:44.686
Maybe you will not find compassion and empathy.

00:25:44.686 --> 00:25:46.372
But it's possible.

00:25:46.648 --> 00:25:48.985
If you say: «Forget it.»

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:53.459
«The relationship is over, I'm not talking to you anymore.» Then there is no hope for progress.

00:25:53.459 --> 00:25:56.077
The same with different countries,

00:25:56.077 --> 00:25:58.185
different races, different religions,

00:25:58.185 --> 00:26:00.909
if you stop talking, then nothing happens.

00:26:00.909 --> 00:26:02.318
That's for sure.

00:26:02.318 --> 00:26:08.167
It's about that for me. And of course you can compare the film to society.

00:26:09.058 --> 00:26:10.932
Viggo, your ...

00:26:10.932 --> 00:26:17.869
You're receiving the Stockholm film festival achievement award now,

00:26:17.869 --> 00:26:25.043
and you're mainly getting that for your fantastic achievements on-screen, 

00:26:25.043 --> 00:26:26.889
but this time you're directing yourself. 

00:26:26.889 --> 00:26:32.252
A lot of people remember you from your collaboration with Peter Jackson, who did «Lord of the Rings».

00:26:32.252 --> 00:26:35.578
What's the most important thing he taught you about directing?

00:26:37.574 --> 00:26:39.638
What I've learnt is

00:26:39.638 --> 00:26:44.661
different from what I've gotten from it.

00:26:44.661 --> 00:26:46.782
I've gotten two things:

00:26:46.782 --> 00:26:55.410
One thing that every person who participated in that film trilogy got was 

00:26:56.362 --> 00:26:59.857
more opportunities to find work.

00:26:59.857 --> 00:27:04.057
Good parts, good opportunities.

00:27:04.057 --> 00:27:07.858
But the most important thing I learned was that 

00:27:11.451 --> 00:27:13.395
to solve problems.

00:27:13.395 --> 00:27:17.614
You know, it was a long shoot, and Peter Jackson had a crew of maybe

00:27:17.614 --> 00:27:20.599
700 people, most of them from New Zealand,

00:27:20.599 --> 00:27:24.974
who had very little experience in making movies, many of them.

00:27:24.974 --> 00:27:28.249
And no experience in making a huge movie like that one.

00:27:28.249 --> 00:27:31.672
So they learned as they went.

00:27:31.672 --> 00:27:37.223
And there were a lot of problems, as there always is when you make a movie.

00:27:37.223 --> 00:27:43.541
And Peter Jackson was amazing in his patience and he was always

00:27:44.063 --> 00:27:46.063
working collectively with people.

00:27:46.247 --> 00:27:52.986
Found ways to solve all kinds of problems. Even if he had to invent a completely new way of filming something.

00:27:52.986 --> 00:27:54.930
«Well, it can be done. We'll figure out a way.»

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:58.850
So it was just that there's always a solution

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:02.061
and you don't have to do it by yourself. In fact,

00:28:02.061 --> 00:28:05.728
it's always better if you do it as a team, you know. So

00:28:05.943 --> 00:28:12.612
that's what I've learnt the most about from Peter Jackson and that experience.

00:28:12.612 --> 00:28:16.127
Where will you store your prize when you receive it?

00:28:19.413 --> 00:28:23.692
Well, here, in the office in my house, I think!

00:28:23.692 --> 00:28:27.924
The prize from Stockholm, the film festival?

00:28:27.924 --> 00:28:33.325
It's ... I've seen it before, it's a horse.

00:28:33.325 --> 00:28:35.263
Like a «Dalahäst».

00:28:35.263 --> 00:28:38.751
But it's made from metal, bronze.

00:28:38.751 --> 00:28:40.840
It must be heavy, I think.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:48.237
So hopefully I won't be flying home.

00:28:48.237 --> 00:28:52.676
It must be driven by car, because it's very heavy. 

00:28:52.676 --> 00:28:55.268
You must promise to have it on display!

00:28:57.448 --> 00:29:01.250
Yes. Yes. It looks beautiful.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:10.195
But it's of course wonderful to get recognition for the work I've done throughout the years.

00:29:10.195 --> 00:29:12.134
I'm a veteran in that sense.

00:29:12.134 --> 00:29:16.891
And it's strange. Because I'm speaking with you as a beginner, a first-time director.

00:29:16.891 --> 00:29:20.334
But on the other hand, I'm getting this prize, which I don't know if it's a 

00:29:20.334 --> 00:29:22.735
hint to say «Okay, you're done»!

00:29:22.735 --> 00:29:24.769
«Make room for others!»

00:29:25.045 --> 00:29:29.778
But I'm not done, I'm stubbornly going to keep going.

00:29:30.576 --> 00:29:36.180
Viggo, it's been lovely to have you on the show, next time we'll meet IRL as it's called.

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.224
Thank you so much, Viggo Mortensen! 

